                <pb facs="0763" n="639" xml:id="pg639"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.21/454: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Colombia</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Goold</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-04-27T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 27,
                                1921—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>19. Department’s 17 April 21, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>In addition to amendments made by Senate which are in exact conformity with those
                    proposed in Mr. Philip’s note of February 27, 1919, and accepted by Mr. Molina’s
                    note of same date,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d609-f1"/> Senate advice and consent to
                    ratifications is given: “with the understanding to be made a part of such treaty
                    and ratification that the provisions of section I of Article I of the treaty
                    granting to the Republic of Colombia free passage through the Panama Canal for
                    its troops, materials of war and ships of war, is not to [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">shall not</hi>] apply in case of war between the
                    Republic of Colombia and any other country.” This makes necessary in both the
                    United States and Colombian ratifications the recital of said understanding and
                    that ratification is subject thereto. Inform Government. Instructions by next
                        pouch.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d609-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
